# The one-argument call (the author 2026-11-11, LMX_blog/2026-11-11.md#call-receivers; the book, call-receivers): an
# ordinary Structure runs over itself as its data. Code declares x at its own root level, so the write lands in Code\x;
# the root's x is another place and stands. Until slice 353 the one-argument form selected a `data` child and refused a
# Structure that had none.
# Plan M11 5v: the declarations of ordinary Structures live in the data part of main; the root holds places and the call
# main().
int: x 5
sub: main () (
    Code:
        int: x
        x := 7
)
    call: Code
main()
int: seen main\data\Code\x
int: root_x x
